From 899813490c05812d8a6c05bbe5f28c2546e09ac8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 18:07:12 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: patch 0240 -- a mouse IRQ doorbell: poll the driver only
 when its state can have changed

WHY: every SDL pump polled the mouse driver with three real-mode INT 33h calls: function 3, and
function 5 for each button (0221/0227). AGS pumps three times a tick, so that is 9 real-mode round
trips a tick even with no input at all.
- On a 486DX2-66 under CWSDPMI, each call costs ~150-170 us inside the game (dosags LAB-MMD.md 33.5).
  That is twice the 83 us a warm back-to-back microbench reports, because a pump's other work evicts
  the DPMI host's and the driver's paths from the 8 KB L1 between calls.
- Measured on that 486 (LAB-MMD.md 28.13, ABBA, MMD): the input phases cost 2.249 ms/tick with
  polling.
- DOSBox-X answers INT 33h inside the emulator, so a fixed-cycles lab sees only a fraction of this
  (0.22 ms/tick).

WHAT: the driver's state -- position, buttons, function 5's press counters -- changes only in its own
IRQ handler or in our INT 33h calls.
- A protected-mode handler is chained on the mouse IRQ's vector (DPMI 0.9 function 0205h via
  __dpmi_set_protected_mode_interrupt_vector). It marks every mouse IRQ: it only sets a flag and
  jumps on to the previous handler -- no sti, no stack switch, no wrapper. This is the pattern of
  DJGPP's own INT 9 keyboard chain.
- A pump runs the polling path -- 0227's, unchanged -- only if the flag was set since the last poll.
  It takes and clears the flag with interrupts off.
- The flag write and the driver's update happen inside the same interrupt, which the main thread
  cannot split. So a pump that skips would have read exactly what the previous poll read. The engine
  receives the same events as with polling, pump for pump, and the press counts still come from
  function 05h itself.
- Our own state-changing calls (warp) set the flag.
- A safety poll runs at least once a second regardless (3 INT 33h a second). It bounds any missed
  doorbell to short staleness that can be detected.

The IRQ comes from INT 33h function 24h (CH = type, CL = IRQ):
- PS/2 only as CH 4 with CL 0: IRQ 12, vector 74h. That is what every CuteMouse release reports
  (LAB-JOURNEYS.md 11.2).
- Bus/serial/InPort/HP only with CL 2-7: vector 08h+CL.
- Anything else, an unimplemented function 24h, or SDL_HINT_DOS_MOUSE_POLL=1: polling every pump, as
  before.

Why not the driver's user routine (function 0Ch)?
- CuteMouse skips user-routine calls while one is running (LAB-JOURNEYS.md 11.1), so counts taken
  there can miss a press.
- A routine pointer left in the driver outlives the process. The PM vector, by contrast, is
  per-client state that the DPMI host drops with the client.

Safety:
- The vector is restored in DOSVESA_QuitMouse, and by an atexit handler as well.
- Everything the handler touches is locked: its code, the flag, the saved vector and libc's DS alias
  (_go32_dpmi_lock_code / _go32_dpmi_lock_data, DPMI 0600h). If any lock fails, there is no
  doorbell: polling as before.
- SDL_DOSMouseBellStats() reports the lock results and the poll, safety-poll and skip counts. These
  are permanent observability, like 0220's SDL_DOSMouseClickStats, not a temporary diagnostic.

Evidence (dosags LAB-MMD.md 28.8-28.13, 34.2-34.4):
- Lab gates, all PASS:
  - determinism guard on MMD, AGS Quest and 5 Days, doorbell vs polling;
  - forced exit, clean in both modes, vector restored;
  - locks = 15 in every doorbell cell;
  - the 486 click recipes, 21/21 presses from host to engine, lost 0, with the doorbell reading the
    driver in ~1.2-1.6% of pumps.
- 486 ABBA: input phases 2.249 -> 0.698 ms/tick (N 0.001, D -1.550, GAIN);
  update_game_once -1.571 ms/tick; the script event is unchanged (control).

Landing review (dosags-worker, 2026-09-25): comments and one include only -- the
per-call cost in the code comment corrected to the measured ~150-170 us in-game (cold)
and ~83 us warm; a note on dos_mouse_bell_old living in .text (written through DS,
fine on DJGPP); #include <dos.h> for disable()/enable(), which were implicitly
declared (a warning here, an error on newer GCC). The object code is identical to the
reviewed patch: SDL_dosevents.c's disassembly and data sections compare equal.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Nz9XeKeVG8Ab7dm8esJsMu
